Relevant columns and their potential usage  

• lap_positions.lap : lap number (to limit to the opening‑laps window 0‑5)  
• lap_positions.position : ranking of each driver on every lap (to observe position changes = overtakes)  
• lap_positions.lap_type : distinguishes “Starting Position – Grid Drop” (lap 0) from normal “Race” laps (useful to isolate start‑related overtakes)  

• results.grid : the official grid slot for every driver (baseline before the lights go out)  

• pit_stops.lap : lap on which every pit stop happened (overtakes triggered by an early stop)  

• retirements.lap : lap on which every retirement happened (overtakes triggered by cars dropping out)  

• retirements.status_id / retirements.retirement_type : lets you confirm it is really a retirement, not DSQ etc.  

Typical conditions you will use  
• WHERE lap BETWEEN 0 AND 5  (for opening laps)  
• WHERE "lap_type" = 'Race'        (exclude the grid‑snapshot, except when you need it)  
• JOIN between lap_positions (two consecutive laps) to see if position changed  
• LEFT JOIN pit_stops / retirements on (race_id, driver_id, lap ≤ 5) to classify the reason for every position change  

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries (LIMITed for inspection) that progress from basic inspection towards the final classification.  
All queries use DISTINCT where inspection rather than counting is the goal.  
Replace the 18 in the examples with the concrete race_id you are analysing.

```sql
-- 1) Inspect the raw positional snapshots of the opening laps
SELECT *
FROM lap_positions
WHERE race_id = 18
  AND lap BETWEEN 0 AND 5
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Check what values appear in lap_type (grid‑snapshot vs race laps)
SELECT DISTINCT lap_type
FROM lap_positions
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) See the official grid (starting order) for that race
SELECT driver_id, grid
FROM results
WHERE race_id = 18
ORDER BY grid
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) First‑lap ranking (after the start) – used to spot start‑related overtakes
SELECT driver_id, position
FROM lap_positions
WHERE race_id = 18
  AND lap = 1
  AND lap_type = 'Race'
ORDER BY position
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Drivers who pitted in the first five laps
SELECT DISTINCT driver_id, lap
FROM pit_stops
WHERE race_id = 18
  AND lap BETWEEN 1 AND 5
ORDER BY lap, driver_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Drivers who retired in the first five laps
SELECT DISTINCT driver_id, lap, retirement_type
FROM retirements
WHERE race_id = 18
  AND lap BETWEEN 1 AND 5
ORDER BY lap
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Position changes between lap 1 and lap 2 (candidate on‑track passes)
SELECT a.driver_id,
       a.position AS lap1_pos,
       b.position AS lap2_pos,
       a.position - b.position AS delta
FROM lap_positions AS a
JOIN lap_positions AS b
  ON a.race_id = b.race_id
 AND a.driver_id = b.driver_id
WHERE a.race_id = 18
  AND a.lap = 1 AND b.lap = 2
  AND a.lap_type = 'Race' AND b.lap_type = 'Race'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Early‑pitters: link the lap‑positions table with pit_stops (lap ≤ 5)
SELECT lp.driver_id, lp.lap, lp.position, ps.lap AS pit_lap
FROM lap_positions AS lp
LEFT JOIN pit_stops AS ps
  ON lp.race_id = ps.race_id
 AND lp.driver_id = ps.driver_id
 AND ps.lap BETWEEN 1 AND 5
WHERE lp.race_id = 18
  AND lp.lap BETWEEN 1 AND 5
  AND lp.lap_type = 'Race'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Position changes that coincide with a retirement on the same lap
SELECT lp.driver_id, lp.lap, r.driver_id AS retired_car
FROM lap_positions AS lp
JOIN retirements AS r
  ON lp.race_id = r.race_id
 AND lp.lap = r.lap
WHERE lp.race_id = 18
  AND lp.lap BETWEEN 1 AND 5
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Sketch of the full classification: count overtakes by cause in the opening laps
SELECT
  SUM(CASE WHEN r.driver_id IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS retirement_overtakes,
  SUM(CASE WHEN ps.driver_id IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS pitstop_overtakes,
  SUM(CASE WHEN lp0.position <> lp1.position AND lp0.lap = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS start_overtakes,
  SUM(CASE WHEN r.driver_id IS NULL
            AND ps.driver_id IS NULL
            AND lp0.lap_type = 'Race'
            AND lp1.lap_type = 'Race'
            AND lp0.position <> lp1.position
            AND lp0.lap BETWEEN 1 AND 4      -- (compare lap N vs N+1, N=1‑4)
       THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)                    AS standard_ontrack_overtakes
FROM lap_positions AS lp0
JOIN lap_positions AS lp1
  ON lp0.race_id = lp1.race_id
 AND lp0.driver_id = lp1.driver_id
 AND lp1.lap = lp0.lap + 1
LEFT JOIN pit_stops  AS ps
  ON lp0.race_id = ps.race_id
 AND lp0.driver_id = ps.driver_id
 AND ps.lap = lp1.lap                       -- pit happened on the target lap
LEFT JOIN retirements AS r
  ON lp0.race_id = r.race_id
 AND r.lap = lp1.lap                        -- retirement happened on the target lap
WHERE lp0.race_id = 18
  AND lp0.lap BETWEEN 0 AND 4;
```

The last query is the basis for the final numbers:  
• retirement_overtakes – passes caused because someone ahead suddenly retired  
• pitstop_overtakes – passes gained because a rival pitted  
• start_overtakes – grid‑vs‑lap1 moves (lights‑out action)  
• standard_ontrack_overtakes – everything else within laps 1‑5 that was not due to a pit stop or retirement.
